              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Code</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Manual&rsquo;s description</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Owner steps from the manual</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E01</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Over load</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Restart; check nothing is jammed; the manual also suggests lubricating (see below)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E02</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Controller error</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check and reconnect the motor line; then motor or controller</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E03</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hardware overload</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Restart; check the power plug and motor connections are not loose</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E04</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Phase error</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check and reconnect the motor cord; then motor or controller</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E05 / E06</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Low voltage / over voltage</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the supply voltage and that the power cord is firmly connected</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E08</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hardware error</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the power plug to motor connection; then controller</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E21</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Save data error</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Power off for 2 minutes and restart; controller if it will not start</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E31</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Over heat</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Restart once the temperature has normalised</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E32 / E33</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor reversal / motor data error</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Service: motor cord (UVW) and motor data</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E91</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Poor communication</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the communication line; then console or controller</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">TR1200B Pro and TR5000B Pro codes (owner&rsquo;s manuals)</h3>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Code</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Manual&rsquo;s description</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Owner steps from the manual</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC-1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Less voltage</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the voltage; make sure the power cord is securely connected</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC-2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Voltage anomaly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Restart the treadmill</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC-3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Over voltage</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the input voltage</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC-4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Over load</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Restart; check nothing is jammed; lubricate; then motor or controller</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC-5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hardware overload</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Restart the machine</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC-6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">MCB error</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The controller needs to be checked</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC-7</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hardware error</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Reconnect the motor cord; check the plug and motor connections; restart after it cools</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC-8</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Switch disconnect</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Switch off; check the Up/Down control connect line is not loose</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Uart</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Communication timeout</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Restart; check for loose connections</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Controller error</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check and reconnect the motor line; then motor or controller</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            LifeSpan&rsquo;s help desk has its own short answers for some of these on older bases. For DC4 it blames too much
            friction, usually too little silicone, and says to lubricate with one ounce, then replace the belt if that does not
            help. For DC6 and DC7 it says the motor control board and running belt need replacing. Those answers go further
            than the Pro manuals&rsquo; first steps, so try the manual&rsquo;s steps first and contact LifeSpan if the code returns.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">E1 on older LifeSpan treadmills: read what the belt did</h3>
          <p>
            LifeSpan&rsquo;s error-code post calls E1 &ldquo;a very broad code&rdquo; meaning the console is not getting a reading from
            the speed sensor, and its help desk diagnoses it by what the belt does before the code appears, with nobody on it:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Runs for more than about 6 seconds, then E1:</strong> the belt needs lubrication. The post explains that the motor pulls too much current and the board shuts down to protect itself.</li>
            <li><strong>Runs for only about 6 seconds:</strong> the speed sensor is not giving a signal, or the main wire harness is damaged.</li>
            <li><strong>Does not move at all, or there is a burning smell:</strong> the motor or motor control board may be damaged.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The same post covers the other older codes: E5 is specific to the TR2000e and TR3000e folding sensors; E6 and E7 are
            incline errors on folding models; for E9, E10 and Uart, power off and on, then check the wires; AC/DC-4 means the
            motor is drawing too much power, so lubricate; and most other AC/DC codes mean switch off for a few minutes, then
            unplug and reseat the cable between the lower board and the console. AC/DC-8 adds checking that the safety key is
            fully inserted. The post&rsquo;s phone number is out of date; use the contact details below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Belt Slipping or Drifting to One Side</h2>
<p>
            Start with the floor. LifeSpan&rsquo;s help desk says the feet on the TR1200-GlowUp are adjustable: turning them
            clockwise shortens them and counter-clockwise lengthens them, so you can level the treadmill on an uneven
            floor. A treadmill that rocks will not hold its belt centred, however carefully you set the bolts. Then check
            when the belt was last lubricated, if your model needs it, because a dry belt can feel like slipping.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Tensioning</h3>
          <p>
            All three current manuals tension the belt from the two bolts at the back of the treadmill with the 6 mm Allen
            wrench supplied, with the treadmill running at 3 mph. The step size differs. The TX6 manual says to turn the right
            and left bolts a quarter turn clockwise. The TR1200B Pro and TR5000B Pro manuals say half a turn. Test, and repeat
            if the belt still slips. All three carry the same caution: do not tighten more than 2 full turns on each side,
            because over-tensioning causes friction and wear on the belt, motor and electronics. Turning one side more than
            the other makes the belt drift, so turn both equally and then check the alignment. Our <a href="/walking-pad-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad belt slipping guide</a> covers the general causes.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Centring</h3>
          <p>
            The TX6 manual&rsquo;s method: start the belt at 3 mph and stand behind the treadmill to see which way it drifts. If it
            drifts left, turn the left bolt a quarter turn clockwise or the right bolt a quarter turn counter-clockwise. If it
            drifts right, turn the left bolt a quarter turn counter-clockwise or the right bolt a quarter turn clockwise. Watch
            it for two minutes and repeat as needed. LifeSpan&rsquo;s help desk suggests checking regularly that the belt is
            centred on the deck and adjusting it if it starts to rub on either side. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring a treadmill belt</a> explains why small turns and patience matter.
          </p>
          <p>
            With an Omni Hub, the console manual does not repeat these steps; for tension and alignment it says to refer to the
            manual for your treadmill base.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lubrication" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Lubrication: Which LifeSpan Models Need It, and How Much</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Whether to lubricate depends on the model, and LifeSpan&rsquo;s documents do not agree on the schedule.</strong> Here is what each says.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">LifeSpan source</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Schedule</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Amount</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TX6 owner&rsquo;s manual</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None: &ldquo;lubrication-free&rdquo; belt, &ldquo;maintenance-free&rdquo;</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">&mdash;</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200B Pro / TR5000B Pro manuals</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Every 50 hours of use or three months</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Never more than 2 oz; no more than 1/2 oz per side in the steps</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Help desk, maintenance article</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">All treadmill bases every 3 months or 40 hours, whichever first</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Blog, &ldquo;How to Lubricate Your Treadmill Belt&rdquo; (2021)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Once every three months</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 oz per application (2 oz when fitting a new belt)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The Pro manuals also contradict themselves on amount. The introduction says to put in one and a half to two ounces
            per application. The numbered steps dispense 0.75 to one ounce per side, then say not to exceed 1/2 ounce per side
            to avoid over-lubrication. The fixed points are clear, though: never more than two ounces, because the manuals say
            over-lubrication can damage the electronics. Use only 100% silicone, and avoid aerosol silicone sprays with additives
            or petroleum distillates. The safe reading is to start at the low end and ask LifeSpan if your belt still drags.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">LifeSpan&rsquo;s method</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Switch off and unplug the treadmill.</li>
            <li>Starting at the front (motor end), slide your hand under the belt to lift it away from the deck.</li>
            <li>Slide the applicator tube under the belt as close to the centre as possible and squeeze gently; the manual says there is no need to slide it along the whole belt.</li>
            <li>Repeat on the other side.</li>
            <li>Plug in and let the treadmill run on its own at no more than 3 mph for 3 to 5 minutes to spread the lubricant, then wipe any excess off the walking surface and side rails.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            LifeSpan&rsquo;s own 100% silicone lubricant was $17 on its store on 28 November 2026 (3.4 oz, which the listing says is
            good for three applications or three months, with three disposable applicators). Our guide to <a href="/how-to-lubricate-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricating a walking pad</a> covers the technique in more detail.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The TX6 exception, and its one contradiction</h3>
          <p>
            The TX6 manual calls its belt lubrication-free, and LifeSpan&rsquo;s spec table lists it as self-lubricating. Yet the
            same manual&rsquo;s E01 overload entry suggests you &ldquo;apply lubricating oil.&rdquo; If a TX6 shows E01 repeatedly,
            contact LifeSpan before putting anything under the belt, rather than guess. A self-lubricating belt treated with the
            wrong product is the kind of thing a warranty review looks at.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When lubricating is not enough</h3>
          <p>
            LifeSpan&rsquo;s error-code post describes a belt that keeps throwing E1 within weeks of being lubricated. It says the
            belt may have &ldquo;glazed,&rdquo; meaning the pores on its underside have closed from friction so it can no longer absorb
            lubricant. In that case the belt needs replacing, and the post warns that carrying on can damage the motor and motor
            control board. It adds that if either of those is replaced, the belt should usually be replaced too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="overheating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Straining, Slowing or Overheating</h2>
<p>
            A desk treadmill runs for hours at low speed, which is hard on the motor if anything adds friction. LifeSpan&rsquo;s
            documents link most straining and overheating to a few causes.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A dry or glazed belt.</strong> The Omni Hub manual&rsquo;s fix for a motor that &ldquo;seems strained or E1 comes up after several minutes&rdquo; is lubrication with 100% non-aerosol silicone.</li>
            <li><strong>Thick carpet.</strong> The TX6 manual says not to place the treadmill on thick carpeting, which can impede air circulation underneath, and recommends cleaning the motor compartment monthly where plush carpet fibres could be pulled in. Our <a href="/walking-pad-overheating/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad overheating guide</a> covers mats and airflow.</li>
            <li><strong>An over-tight belt.</strong> All three current manuals warn that over-tensioning causes friction and wear on the belt, motor and electronics.</li>
            <li><strong>Power supply.</strong> TX6 codes E05 and E06 and Pro codes DC-1 and DC-3 are voltage errors, which is one more reason to avoid extension cords and shared power strips.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If the belt moves slower than the speed you set, LifeSpan&rsquo;s help desk does not offer a home fix. It says to contact
            its tech support. None of the current pads has a cooling fan; LifeSpan&rsquo;s help desk lists only three folding
            treadmills with one (TR3000i, TR4000i and TR5500iM). That means airflow under the deck matters more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-returns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warranty, Returns and How to Contact LifeSpan</h2>
<p>
            <strong>LifeSpan&rsquo;s warranty on its under-desk treadmills is 10 years on the frame, 2 years on parts and 1 year
            on labour, but you must register within 60 days and get any repair pre-authorised.</strong> The TX6 manual prints
            those terms for the TX6, TR1000B, TR1200B and TR5000B, valid in North America, and LifeSpan&rsquo;s Policies page (read
            28 November 2026) lists the same frame, parts and labour terms.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the warranty requires</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Register within 60 days.</strong> The Policies page says LifeSpan will not honour warranties for unregistered products or those registered more than 60 days after purchase.</li>
            <li><strong>Where you bought it.</strong> If you bought from another retailer or website, the page says to contact them for warranty claims; Amazon orders shipped and sold by LifeSpan go to LifeSpan directly.</li>
            <li><strong>No home repairs without approval.</strong> All repairs must be pre-authorised. The page says repairs by anyone other than a LifeSpan-authorised technician void the warranty, and that hiring someone or installing parts yourself is a voiding condition. The manuals add that non-LifeSpan parts void it.</li>
            <li><strong>Original owner, home use.</strong> The warranty is non-transferable and limited to non-commercial home use. Commercial or rental use voids it.</li>
            <li><strong>Replacement units.</strong> If LifeSpan replaces the whole treadmill, the replacement carries only a 30-day warranty, and the original 10-year, 2-year and 1-year cover is treated as fulfilled.</li>
            <li><strong>Labour charge.</strong> If parts are shipped under warranty, the page says the customer pays a minimal labour charge.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Returns: the 20% plus $350 rule</h3>
          <p>
            LifeSpan&rsquo;s Policies page says it stopped accepting 30-day returns on 1 February 2025. For any US order cancelled or
            returned after it has been marked as shipped, received or not, and for any order refused on delivery, LifeSpan deducts a
            20% restocking fee and a $350 return shipping fee. Returns need written pre-approval, the original packaging and an
            unedited unboxing video recorded on receipt. Purchases from 25 November to 15 December are eligible only for exchange or
            store credit until 15 January, and sale items are credit or exchange only. Our <a href="/how-much-does-a-lifespan-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LifeSpan cost guide</a> works out what that means in dollars for each model, and our <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a> page compares other brands.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Parts bought separately</h3>
          <p>
            Parts bought from LifeSpan have their own short warranty: 30 days for non-electronic parts and 15 days for electronic
            parts, from delivery. You need a Return Authorization number, the RA expires after 15 days, and LifeSpan says &ldquo;if you
            install it, you own it&rdquo; for refunds. It recommends having an authorised technician diagnose an out-of-warranty fault
            on site before ordering parts.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to reach LifeSpan</h3>
          <p>
            LifeSpan&rsquo;s Contact Us page (read 28 November 2026) gives (702) 996-1986 and support@lifespanfitness.com, Monday to
            Friday, 8 am to 5 pm Pacific. It says email is its primary support channel and to expect a reply in 1 to 3 business
            days on technical questions. It also runs a help desk with chat. For any warranty claim, the Policies page asks for:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>the model number and serial number;</li>
            <li>the name and phone number the product is registered under;</li>
            <li>proof and date of purchase;</li>
            <li>a picture or video of the problem. A short clip of the belt stopping or the code appearing saves a round of emails.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Several LifeSpan documents still print older numbers: the 2021 error-code post gives 801.973.9993 x4, and one help desk
            article gives 801-973-9993. Use the Contact Us page details. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains how frame, parts and labour cover work in practice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Simple LifeSpan Desk Treadmill Routine</h2>
<p>
            Most of the faults above come down to friction, dust, power and settings. Putting LifeSpan&rsquo;s manuals and help desk
            together gives a short routine. Unplug before any cleaning or adjustment; the manuals say to unplug before removing the
            motor cover.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">How often</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Task</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">LifeSpan source</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">After each use</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wipe the belt, painted parts and display; mild detergent at times, never acidic cleaners</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TX6 and Pro manuals</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Monthly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check connecting screws and parts; clean the motor compartment if carpet fibres can get in; check the power cord</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TX6 manual</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Regularly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the belt is centred and not rubbing either side</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Help desk</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">50 hours or 3 months (help desk: 40 hours or 3 months)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubricate with 100% silicone, Pro and older bases only</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Pro manuals; help desk</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Once, at setup</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Register within 60 days; note the serial number; set Speed Limit and Intelli-Guard to suit you</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Policies page; console manuals</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Placement prevents the rest. Put the treadmill on a flat, hard floor or a thin mat rather than thick carpet. Give it a
            grounded, non-GFI wall outlet within reach of its 6-foot cord. Keep 1.6 to 3.3 feet of clearance around it, which is
            the TX6 manual&rsquo;s safety figure. If you are still choosing a setup, our <a href="/best-treadmill-desks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill desks</a> guide compares LifeSpan with its rivals.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes Troubleshooting a LifeSpan</h2>
          <p>Most LifeSpan faults are settings or simple checks. These four make them take longer or cost more.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Treating Intelli-Guard as a fault</h3>
              <p>A belt that glides to a stop when you pause is the feature working. If it stops while you walk, LifeSpan’s fix is to turn Intelli-Guard off in settings, not to call for a new motor.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Plugging into a GFI outlet or extension cord</h3>
              <p>LifeSpan’s Pro manuals say not to use a GFI outlet or circuit and not to use an extension cord, and the TX6 manual rules out multi-hole sockets and adapters. Use a grounded wall outlet within reach of the 6-foot cord.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Lubricating the wrong way, or the wrong model</h3>
              <p>The TX6 manual calls its belt lubrication-free, and the Pro manuals cap lubricant at two ounces and rule out aerosol sprays. Over-lubrication can damage the electronics, so check your own manual first.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Opening it up before calling LifeSpan</h3>
              <p>LifeSpan’s warranty requires pre-authorised repairs and says self-installed parts or unauthorised repairs void it. Register within 60 days and contact LifeSpan before taking the motor cover off.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The fix, in short</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">If a LifeSpan desk treadmill stops, check Intelli-Guard first. If it will not start, push the safety key fully in, reseat the
            9-pin console cable and plug it into a grounded, non-GFI wall outlet with no extension cord, then power-cycle at the
            base switch.</strong> Check the speed limit before assuming a 2 mph treadmill is broken, and look up any code in the
            table for your own model: E01 to E91 on the TX6, DC codes on the TR1200B Pro and TR5000B Pro, and the 2021 list on
            older bases.
          </p>
          <p>
            For belt problems, level the feet, lubricate with 100% silicone only if your model takes it, then tension and centre
            at 3 mph in small, equal turns, never more than two full turns per side. Register the treadmill within 60 days, and
            contact LifeSpan before opening it: unauthorised repairs void the warranty, and returns cost 20% plus $350 once an
            order ships. Our <a href="/lifespan-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">LifeSpan treadmills guide</a> covers each model, and our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill troubleshooting guide</a> covers every brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/">Treadmill Troubleshooting by Symptom</a></li>
