        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why does my LifeSpan treadmill keep stopping?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually Intelli-Guard, which stops the belt when it does not detect steps: after more than 60 seconds on the TX6, TR1200B Pro and TR5000B Pro, and after 20 seconds on older DT desks. If it stops while you are walking, LifeSpan says the treadmill is not picking up your steps and to turn Intelli-Guard off in settings. A TX6 also stops itself after 99 minutes 59 seconds; wait two minutes and restart.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I turn off Intelli-Guard on a LifeSpan treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On the Omni Hub, tap Set to open settings; Intelli-Guard is the first item, and you change it with the arrows. On 2016 and later DT5 and DT7 desks and 2018 and later DT3 desks, pause, hold Stop/Reset then Change Display for one second each, press Up to reach "Guard", press Start, set it to OFF and press Start to save. The TX6 manual gives no remote setting for it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why won’t my LifeSpan treadmill go faster than 2 mph?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Check the speed limit. The Omni Hub's Speed Limit setting can be 4.0 or 2.0 mph, and older DT consoles default to a 2.0 mph maximum. On a TX6, holding + and − together on the remote locks the speed at 2 mph, and the manual says to unlock it before connecting a desk console. A TX6 on an Omni Hub is limited to 4 mph.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What does E1 mean on a LifeSpan treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On older LifeSpan treadmills, E1 means the console is not getting a speed sensor reading, and LifeSpan diagnoses it by belt behaviour. If the belt runs for more than about 6 seconds first, it needs lubricating. If it runs for only about 6 seconds, the speed sensor needs attention. If it does not move at all, the motor control board may need replacing. On the TR1200B Pro and TR5000B Pro, E1 is listed as a controller error.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How often should I lubricate a LifeSpan treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on the model. The TX6 manual says its belt is lubrication-free. The TR1200B Pro and TR5000B Pro manuals say every 50 hours of use or three months, never more than two ounces. LifeSpan's help desk says every 3 months or 40 hours for its treadmill bases. Use 100% silicone only, not aerosol sprays.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I return a LifeSpan treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only with LifeSpan's written pre-approval, and it is expensive. Its Policies page says 30-day returns ended on 1 February 2025, and any US order returned or refused after shipping is charged a 20% restocking fee plus $350 return shipping. Orders from 25 November to 15 December are exchange or store credit only until 15 January.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is LifeSpan’s customer service number?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              LifeSpan's Contact Us page listed (702) 996-1986 and support@lifespanfitness.com on 28 November 2026, Monday to Friday, 8 am to 5 pm Pacific, and says email is its main support channel. Have the model, serial number, registered name and phone number, proof of purchase and a photo or video of the problem ready.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
